---
/* The page a visitor gets for a URL this site does not have.

   It has to exist as a file. A static host with no 404.html answers an unknown
   path with 200 and the home page — Cloudflare Pages does exactly that, so a
   mistyped link looked like a working page and search engines were free to
   index every typo as a copy of the home page. This file is what makes the
   host answer 404 and say so.

   Its words are hard-coded, and this is the one page in the template where
   that is right. Everything else takes its prose from src/content so the owner
   can edit it in place — but nobody reaches a 404 in review mode to edit it,
   and an owner who broke this page would hear about it from a visitor. Chrome,
   not content.

   No styles either, on purpose: the template ships machinery and no face
   (styles.css says why), so this page wears whatever the site's design becomes.
   When it gets one, style it with logical properties — margin-inline,
   padding-block, inset-inline — like every other page in the fleet, so a
   right-to-left locale costs nothing. */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

/* Canonical to itself, never to "/". Pointing it at the home page would tell a
   crawler that the missing URL *is* the home page, which is the confusion this
   file exists to end. No editCollection/editEntry, so the layout's inline
   editing correctly finds nothing to edit here. */
---

<Base
  title="Page not found"
  description="This page does not exist."
  ogDescription="This page does not exist."
  canonical="/404"
>
  <main>
    <h1>Page not found</h1>
    <p>That page does not exist — it may have moved, or the link may be wrong.</p>
    <p><a href="/">Go to the home page</a></p>
  </main>
</Base>
